                "word": "sferics",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fe8aee93-0b33-341c-972b-07eec7915a4c",
                        "entry_id": "5b32c276-ecf7-3743-9416-a502d2032561",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Electromagnetic pulses caused by atmospheric phenomena, such as lightning. plural of sferic"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "plural-only"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4b90c804-0452-426c-9723-ebe039a2ef9a",
                                "sense_id": "fe8aee93-0b33-341c-972b-07eec7915a4c",
                                "text": "A Sferics Locating System has been developed to determine the geographical location of lightning strokes..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4e2ff9db-348c-4741-8a70-562d44086c25",
                                "sense_id": "fe8aee93-0b33-341c-972b-07eec7915a4c",
                                "text": "...an artificial sferics generator was used as a known source of EM pulses."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "42905173-1feb-4b26-aeb3-a6b0be909582",
                                "sense_id": "fe8aee93-0b33-341c-972b-07eec7915a4c",
                                "text": "1987: Barbara Tufty, 1001 Questions Answered About Hurricanes, Tornadoes and Other Natural Air Disasters http://books.google.com/books?vid=ISBN0486254550&id=LpVoN0gmVKcC&pg=RA2-PA38&lpg=RA2-PA38&dq=sferics&sig=BapiarKgnm1NVr9NDBl1Xcgtp-A\nResearchers have thought that possibly these sferics could be used to track hurricanes—but so far, they have been only marginally successful."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ec7b7c48-f849-408f-9a3c-5b07a6a735ea",
                                "sense_id": "fe8aee93-0b33-341c-972b-07eec7915a4c",
                                "text": "Sferics are classified normally by the frequency of the receiver used to detect them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "22320005-2148-4342-934e-ff0fd961f853",
                                "sense_id": "fe8aee93-0b33-341c-972b-07eec7915a4c",
                                "text": "This circuit generates a bipolar pulse waveform that closely approximates the main features of sferics."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "359c74a4-c8ae-4691-b3f8-df9214184161",
                                "sense_id": "fe8aee93-0b33-341c-972b-07eec7915a4c",
                                "text": "The second common theoretical approach of the propagation of sferics involves viewing the Earth-ionosphere cavity as a waveguide..."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bd0c912b-4b6c-3abf-855b-2b98da83f3e9",
                        "entry_id": "5b32c276-ecf7-3743-9416-a502d2032561",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Radio interference caused by sferics; atmospherics."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "plural-only"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b360a9cc-56ee-4373-a30b-d6255c84fcf7",
                                "sense_id": "bd0c912b-4b6c-3abf-855b-2b98da83f3e9",
                                "text": "In the identification of sources of short whistlers, the spectral characteristics of the sferics can often be used to establish many sferics as doubtful..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "57276461-3b99-45ce-b327-7fbfd488e329",
                                "sense_id": "bd0c912b-4b6c-3abf-855b-2b98da83f3e9",
                                "text": "As it turned out, the whistler was only the first of a family of sferics whose taxonomy was to include clicks, hooks, risers, nose-whistlers and one like a warbling of birds called the dawn chorus...."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
